== a. one source that is right 90% of the time, and M sources that are right 50% of the time (pure noise).
      Every source makes one polar claim on each of 4000 propositions; 20% of them are also sensed. ==
   M =  0 noise sources;  learned hit rates: good 0.911
     design, tau = expectation of trust tie       right side of 1/2: 90.1%   Brier 0.116   mean c 0.477
     corrected, rho = chance-corrected            right side of 1/2: 90.1%   Brier 0.120   mean c 0.451
     exact Bayes with the learned hit rates       right side of 1/2: 90.1%   Brier 0.089   mean c 0.823
   M =  3 noise sources;  learned hit rates: good 0.915, noise mean 0.489
     design, tau = expectation of trust tie       right side of 1/2: 80.4%   Brier 0.176   mean c 0.704
     corrected, rho = chance-corrected            right side of 1/2: 90.1%   Brier 0.120   mean c 0.454
     exact Bayes with the learned hit rates       right side of 1/2: 90.1%   Brier 0.089   mean c 0.830
   M = 10 noise sources;  learned hit rates: good 0.886, noise mean 0.496
     design, tau = expectation of trust tie       right side of 1/2: 59.3%   Brier 0.218   mean c 0.854
     corrected, rho = chance-corrected            right side of 1/2: 89.4%   Brier 0.131   mean c 0.471
     exact Bayes with the learned hit rates       right side of 1/2: 89.4%   Brier 0.095   mean c 0.768
   M = 30 noise sources;  learned hit rates: good 0.914, noise mean 0.503
     design, tau = expectation of trust tie       right side of 1/2: 57.1%   Brier 0.236   mean c 0.941
     corrected, rho = chance-corrected            right side of 1/2: 90.2%   Brier 0.140   mean c 0.566
     exact Bayes with the learned hit rates       right side of 1/2: 90.2%   Brier 0.090   mean c 0.817
   -> a source at chance has tau = 1/2, so it is counted as half an observation per claim.  Ten such sources
      drown a 90% source (accuracy 90% -> 59%) and the mind reads conviction c ~ 0.85 on beliefs that
      are noise.  With rho = 2A - 1 the noise weighs nothing and c stays at what the good source can carry.

== b. the class 'told' holds an honest teller (right 95%) and a lying teller (right 5%), equally talkative.
      Six class slots (§1, §10: w+[6], w-[6]) leave room for ONE tau_told. ==
   learned: tau_told (one class slot) = 0.495;   per source: honest 0.944, liar 0.047
     design, one slot for the class 'told'        right side of 1/2:  4.5%   Brier 0.256   mean c 0.498
       (undecided, e = 1/2 exactly: 90.8% of the propositions: the two tellers cancel)
     design GIVEN per-source slots, tau = hit rate right side of 1/2: 95.0%   Brier 0.099   mean c 0.498
     corrected, per source, rho = 2A - 1          right side of 1/2: 95.0%   Brier 0.093   mean c 0.470
   -> with class slots the mind learns NOTHING from being told (the liar's evidence cannot be separated after it
      was added into the same slot, and it cannot be re-weighted later).  'Trust in the teller' needs evidence
      indexed by source unit.  Per-source tau = hit rate still counts the liar at 0.05 per claim; rho does not.

== c. birth value and gauge. ==
   an evidence-free trust tie has expectation e = 0.50  ->  at birth tau_sensed = tau_told = tau_imagined = 0.5
   first hearing by the design's own definition: W = 0.5 -> c = 0.333   (the text says 0.3/1.3 = 0.231)
   first SIGHTING by the same definition:        W = 0.5 -> c = 0.333   (a sighting and a hearsay weigh the same)
   tau_told = 0.3 needs evidence on the trust tie, e.g. W- = 2/3 with W+ = 0: e = 0.300
   The trust tie of 'sensed' is refereed by nothing (the text names sensed evidence as the referee of the others).
   If C3 referees it by itself in a world whose regularities hold with frequency p, tau_sensed -> ~p:
     p = 0.5: tau_sensed = 0.500  -> every sensed count is scaled by 0.50; a tie seen 10 times reads c = 0.833 instead of 0.909
     p = 0.7: tau_sensed = 0.700  -> every sensed count is scaled by 0.70; a tie seen 10 times reads c = 0.875 instead of 0.909
     p = 0.9: tau_sensed = 0.900  -> every sensed count is scaled by 0.90; a tie seen 10 times reads c = 0.900 instead of 0.909
   f = W+/W is unchanged when all tau are scaled by a common factor, c is not: the unit of evidence is undefined
   until one class is fixed (gauge).

== d. ONE teller (tau_told = 0.3) repeats the same statement n times. ==
   n     design W   c       | corrected W = par(n, rho/(1-rho))   c      | against ONE own contrary sighting: design f, e | corrected f, e
      1       0.3   0.231   |        0.300                      0.231   |          0.23  0.35                  |   0.23  0.35
      2       0.6   0.375   |        0.353                      0.261   |          0.37  0.42                  |   0.26  0.36
      4       1.2   0.545   |        0.387                      0.279   |          0.55  0.53                  |   0.28  0.37
     10       3.0   0.750   |        0.411                      0.291   |          0.75  0.70                  |   0.29  0.38
    100      30.0   0.968   |        0.427                      0.299   |          0.97  0.95                  |   0.30  0.38
   1000     300.0   0.997   |        0.428                      0.300   |          1.00  1.00                  |   0.30  0.38
   -> in the design four repetitions outweigh one own sighting, and every repetition is felt as relief (§5:
      c rises).  With the series coupling a belief known from one source alone can never exceed c = rho.

== e. self-trust earned by the base rate.  In this world 90% of all (instance, property) ties hold anyway.
      The mind's constructions are NOT informative: it concludes 'i -> v' for random pairs.  Later the senses
      check them. ==
   design: tau('my own construction') = 0.907  -> every construction is written at 0.91 of a sensed observation
           a never-observed conclusion after ONE construction: W = 0.45, c = 0.31; after 10 idle cycles see evm_s3 B
   corrected: base rate b = 0.90, precision A = 0.907 -> rho = (A - b)/(1 - b) = 0.069
           the same conclusion: W = 0.069, c = 0.064, e = 0.906 (the base rate; nothing was learned by constructing)
   -> a hit rate is not a reliability.  The top prediction of a mind that has learned the base rates is confirmed
      most of the time; counting those hits as trust and then multiplying NEW evidence by it counts the base
      rate twice.
